            <ArticleTitle>Acute back pain: a psychological analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>429-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A cross-sectional evaluation of 117 people who sustained acute back injuries was undertaken within 15 days of the first report of the pain. The subjects showed no discal or neural signs and had not experienced previous episodes of back or neck pain. All subjects were given a structured interview and filled in a series of psychological evaluation instruments. Results show acute pain reactions to be comparable to those seen in chronic pain groups. The predominant emotion is one of frustration rather than anxiety or depression and considerable behavioural disruption is evident from this early point. The extent to which these data undermine the model of gradual evolution of chronic back pain problems is discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
